// S~S  SimpleSal signal starts  S~S

// [a Welcome to SimpleSal.note]        a search in source files will find a reference to this file.
//
   =================================================================================================
   This note describes "what's in that note?" and "where is that note?" for notes in the ssDocs tree.
   =================================================================================================
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   SimpleSal is implemented using a simplistic building-block programming language: what it calls ssHL-language.
       The name that trips off the end of the tongue is shorthand for "SimpleSal Host Language".
       ssHL-language is simplistic because it only uses integers, characters, and pointers as building blocks.
//
   Build-time considerations:
       ssHL-language is defined as the subset of the C language (look it up) that is recognized and usable
       COMPILED within a Host Language such as Arduino's C++, microPython, an RTOS task using K&R C.
       Host languages usually have facilities for including software written in other languages,
       or in the case of C++, including software written exclusively in the legacy form of K&R C,
       or linking executable components together using common forms and organizational methods.
//
   Target Run-time considerations:
       Once executable content is generated: C, C++, microPython, ssHL-language....all are machine language.
       Because SimpleSal is focused on the mesa, which is focused on a processor executing only
       machine language, there are no references in SimpleSal to the Host environment at Run time.
//
   SimpleSal's fundamental philosophy:
        better to create a thousand poems from seven words,
        than to create seven poems from a thousand words.
//
   ssHL-language has a useful feature that allows the software to refer to invented ideas and abstractions.
   All software is expressing something in a language impacted by the Application, Host, or devices.
   This is useful for abstracting device or host technical terminology into application terminology,
   and, for abstracting application teminology into device or host technical terminology.
//
   The narrative flow uses objects like "gate sensor back yard" and "gate is open" or "lock gate".
   A device data sheet will say very complex things expressed from within the language of the device.
   A mapping such as "when I want to say this or must say this, I'm going to say it this way"
   allows the software's narrative flow to refer to objects defined by the Application.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   As an example, the use of the exact type name "bool" has never made sense to SimpleSal.  In order
   to make decisions while software is executing, boolean logic is supported by the Host Language.
   The distance away from the complete, and just as immediately readable, "boolean" requires that
   the reader make a translation, at least a lookup, to load the "boolean" meaning in the mind.
   The beauty of this idea is that any user of SimpleSal is free to rename anything in the same way.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// S~S  SimpleSal signal ends
